.TH typo "" "Command"
.SH NAME
typo \- detect possible typographical and spelling errors
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtypo [\-nrs] [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
typo -- detect possible typographical and spelling errors
Usage:	typo [ -nrs ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-n	Do not use built-in English statistics or dictionary
	-r	Raw; do not remove nroff commands from the input
	-s	Produce 'digrams' and 'trigrams' files (maintenance only)
.HE
.B typo
looks for likely typographical errors in an English-language
document by a statistical test, rather than by matching words against
a fixed dictionary.
For each word, it computes an index of peculiarity from the digram
(letter-pair) and trigram (letter-triple) frequencies of that word,
measured against the digram and trigram frequencies of the whole
document (helped, unless
.B \-n
is given, by built-in frequency tables and a small exception
dictionary of common technical words).
Words that use letter combinations rarely seen elsewhere in the
document score a high index and are the ones most likely to be
misspellings.
.PP
.B typo
reads each
.I file
named (or the standard input if none is given), extracts the
alphabetic words, folds them to lower case, and discards any word
found in its exception dictionary (\fB/usr/dict/dict\fR).
It sorts the remaining distinct words by descending index of
peculiarity and prints each one preceded by its index.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Do not load the built-in digram and trigram statistics or the
exception dictionary.
More words will be printed, and their indices of peculiarity will be
less reliable, especially for a short document.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Raw mode: do not strip lines beginning with `.' or remove the
.B nroff
escape character `\e', which
.B typo
otherwise assumes surround formatting requests rather than text.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Instead of printing an index of peculiarity for each word, write the
digram and trigram frequency counts observed in the input to files
named
.B digrams
and
.B trigrams
in the current directory.
These are the same format as
.B /usr/dict/digrams
and
.B /usr/dict/trigrams
and are meant for regenerating the built-in tables, not for everyday
use.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/usr/dict/dict\fR	exception dictionary
\fB/usr/dict/digrams\fR	built-in digram frequencies
\fB/usr/dict/trigrams\fR	built-in trigram frequencies
\fB/tmp/typo*\fR	temporary file holding the unsorted word list
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B sort
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B typo
complains and exits if it cannot open an input
.IR file ,
if it runs out of memory building the trigram tables, or if it cannot
locate the
.B sort
command it pipes the word list through.
.SH NOTES
.B typo
sorts its intermediate word list by invoking
.B sort
in a child process; this must be found on
.BR PATH .
